        <div id="read-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Read a Treadmill Warranty</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill warranty is several warranties of different lengths stacked together, and the longest
            one usually covers the part least likely to fail. Read them in this order:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Parts.</strong> Controller boards, incline motors, rollers, belts and consoles are what
            actually break. Ten years, as on the Spirits, is exceptional; five, as on the <a href="/horizon-7-4-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Horizon 7.4 AT</a>, is
            strong; one, as on the Horizon T101, is the minimum.</li>
            <li><strong>Labour.</strong> Once labour ends you pay for the technician even when the part is free.
            Most home warranties give one year; Horizon&rsquo;s 7.4 and 7.8 AT give two.</li>
            <li><strong>Motor.</strong> A lifetime motor warranty is now common in this price band and is a good
            sign, but drive motors are not the most common failure.</li>
            <li><strong>Frame and deck.</strong> Welded steel frames rarely fail. A lifetime frame on its own tells
            you little; a lifetime deck, as on the Spirits, is rarer.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Part</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">How often it fails</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What to look for</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Electronics (controller, console)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The most common expensive repair</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3+ years on parts</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt and deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wear items; depend on lubrication and use</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Deck cover, or a long parts term</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Less common, costly when it happens</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime is now standard here</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Frame</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Rare</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime is a given, not a reason to buy</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains exclusions, registration and what voids cover, and our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a> shows which parts wear first.
          </p>
        </div>

