        <div id="claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Make a Treadmill Warranty Claim</h2>
<p>
            A warranty is only as useful as the claim you can make on it. The steps are similar across brands:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Find the model and serial number</strong> on the sticker on the frame, usually near the
            front or the power inlet. Model names on the console are reused across years; the serial number
            is what the maker checks.</li>
            <li><strong>Have proof of purchase ready</strong>, with the date and the seller&rsquo;s name. This is
            where buying from the brand or an authorised seller matters.</li>
            <li><strong>Record the fault.</strong> A short phone video of the symptom, any error message and
            what you have already tried. Many makers diagnose from a video before sending a part.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask whether the claim is parts-only or in-home service.</strong> Inside the labour term a
            technician should come to you; after it, you may receive the part and fit it yourself or pay for the
            visit.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the box for the first weeks.</strong> Early faults are sometimes handled as a
            return rather than a repair.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            For common faults you can rule out yourself before calling, our <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill troubleshooting guide</a> works through each symptom.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="extended" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Are Extended Treadmill Warranties Worth It?</h2>
<p>
            Retailers often offer a protection plan at checkout. Whether it is worth it depends on the gap it
            fills. On a machine with one year of parts and labour, a plan that covers in-home repairs for three
            or four years can be reasonable value, because controller and console failures tend to arrive after the
            first year. On a machine that already gives five or ten years on parts, the plan mostly duplicates the
            maker&rsquo;s cover and adds little beyond labour.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before buying one, read who honours it (the retailer, the maker or a third-party insurer), whether it
            includes in-home labour, and whether it excludes wear items such as belts and decks. Often the cheaper
            route to the same protection is simply buying a model with a longer manufacturer warranty, which is the
            point of this list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-voids" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Can Void a Treadmill Warranty</h2>
<p>
            A long warranty is only worth what you can claim on. The common ways owners lose cover:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Buying from an unauthorised seller.</strong> Several makers only honour warranties for
            machines bought from them or an authorised dealer. Check who the Amazon seller is before you order.</li>
            <li><strong>Not registering.</strong> Some warranties need registration within a set time. Do it on
            the day of delivery and keep the receipt.</li>
            <li><strong>Skipping maintenance.</strong> A dry deck strains the motor and controller. Follow the
            manual&rsquo;s lubrication guidance; our <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication guide</a> covers how.</li>
            <li><strong>Commercial or shared use.</strong> Residential warranties exclude gyms and, often,
            apartment-building fitness rooms.</li>
            <li><strong>The wrong outlet.</strong> Horizon&rsquo;s spec tables say its 7-series treadmills are not
            compatible with GFCI outlets. Read the electrical requirements before you plug in.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-paying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is a Longer Warranty Worth Paying For?</h2>
<p>
            Usually, at the step from one year of parts cover to three or five. A single controller board or
            incline motor replacement out of warranty can cost a meaningful share of a <a href="/best-budget-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>budget treadmill</a>&rsquo;s
            price, plus a technician visit. Our <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a> sets out typical repair costs.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Comparison</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Extra price</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Extra cover</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon 7.0 AT → 7.4 AT</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $565 on Amazon</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">+2 years parts, +1 year labour, 22-inch deck</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon T101 Connect → 7.0 AT</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $158 on Amazon</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">+2 years parts, 60-inch deck, 12 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon 7.4 AT → Spirit XT285</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $800 on Amazon</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">+5 years parts, lifetime deck, but 1 year labour</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The T101-to-7.0 step stands out: for about $158 more on Amazon you gain two years of parts cover
            and a running deck. The step to a Spirit buys the longest parts cover available at home but gives
            back a year of labour against the Horizon 7.4 AT. For heavy daily use, or a household with several
            users, the Spirit terms are the safest; for most households, the Horizon 7.4 AT is the balance.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buying used changes the sum.</strong> Many home treadmill warranties cover only the original
            purchaser, so a second-hand machine may come with no cover at all, however long the original terms. Ask
            the maker whether its warranty transfers before you pay for a used treadmill on the strength of its
            warranty, and price a used machine as if any repair will be yours. Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill buying checklist</a> covers what to inspect, and <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new vs used</a> sets out when the saving is worth the risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes Comparing Treadmill Warranties</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from reading the headline instead of the parts and labour lines.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying on “lifetime frame”</h3>
              <p>Frames almost never fail. A lifetime frame with one year on parts protects you far less than a ten-year frame with five years on parts. Compare the parts and labour terms first.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring labour</h3>
              <p>After the labour term ends, every repair includes a technician’s call-out even when the part is free. Two years of labour, as on Horizon’s 7.4 and 7.8 AT, is worth noticing.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying from an unknown seller</h3>
              <p>Some makers only honour warranties for authorised sellers. On Amazon, check who the seller is before ordering, and keep the receipt.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not registering on delivery</h3>
              <p>Registration often has a deadline. Register the day it arrives, note the serial number from the frame sticker, and file the receipt with the manual.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For the longest warranty, buy a Spirit XT485 or XT285</strong>: lifetime frame,
            motor and deck plus ten years on parts. For the best warranty under $1,500, the Horizon 7.4 AT gives
            five years on parts and two years of labour; under $1,000, the Horizon 7.0 AT gives lifetime motor cover
            and three years on parts.
          </p>
          <p>
            Read past the word “lifetime” to the parts and labour lines, buy from an authorised seller, and
            register on delivery. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">warranty guide</a> covers what voids cover and how to claim.
          </p>
        </div>

